<title>GERMANY: EU end-March intervention grain stocks 1.66 mln T.</title>
<headline>EU end-March intervention grain stocks 1.66 mln T.</headline>
<dateline>BONN 1997-04-14</dateline>
<text>
<p>Public intervention grain stocks in the European Union at the end of March totalled 1.66 million tonnes, some 60 percent below the level a year earlier, a weekly circular from the German agriculture ministry said.</p>
<p>More than half -- 832,800 tonnes -- were rye.</p>
<p>Germany accounted for 87 percent of the total, at 1.45 million (3.76 million a year earlier), mostly because it stores large volumes of feed rye.</p>
<p>Ireland was second, with 70,500 tonnes stored.</p>
<p>Feed grain offers in Ireland had risen in the wake of the mad cow crisis, which reduced feeding requirements, the Bonn farm ministry report said.</p>
<p>France, the Netherlands, Belgium, Austria and Portugal had no intervention grain reserve.</p>
<p>-Vera Eckert, Hamburg newsroom, +49-40-41903275</p>
